    <!-- Help / FAQ -->
    <string name="help_title">Help and FAQ</string>
    <string name="help_search_hint">Search help articles</string>
    <string name="article_not_found_title">Phone not found during discovery</string>
    <string name="article_not_found_body">1. Make sure both phones have MorseLink open on the pairing screen.\n2. Keep both phones unlocked and within a few meters.\n3. On newer phones, grant the Nearby devices permission.\n4. Try switching to Wi-Fi searching: both devices must join the same Wi-Fi network, or one phone can start Share with PC to create a hotspot and the other joins it.\n5. Still stuck? Run the Connection Doctor below.</string>
    <string name="article_stall_title">Transfer stalls partway through</string>
    <string name="article_stall_body">1. Pause and resume the transfer from the queue — MorseLink resumes from the exact byte where it stopped on the Wi-Fi transport.\n2. Keep the sender screen unlocked for the duration.\n3. If battery saver is on, transfers may be throttled — plug in or disable it.\n4. A very long transfer can also pause if Wi-Fi drops; resume when you are back in range.</string>
    <string name="article_browser_title">Browser cannot connect to WebShare</string>
    <string name="article_browser_title_alt">Browser cannot connect to WebShare</string>
    <string name="article_browser_body">1. Check the other device is on the same network: either the same Wi-Fi, or joined to your phone hotspot.\n2. Type the address exactly as shown, including the number after the colon.\n3. The address contains a session key — if you restarted WebShare, use the new address.\n4. Some public Wi-Fi networks block device-to-device traffic. Use hotspot mode instead.</string>
    <string name="article_apk_title">Received APK will not install</string>
    <string name="article_apk_body">1. Android asks to allow this app to install unknown apps — allow it for MorseLink in the dialog.\n2. If the install button does nothing, the APK may be for a different processor type.\n3. MorseLink never modifies received files — the APK is byte-identical to what was sent.</string>
    <string name="article_hotspot_title">Hotspot will not turn on</string>
    <string name="article_hotspot_body">1. On Android 8 and newer, hotspot mode is created by the system automatically — if it fails, location services must be on.\n2. On Android 7 and older, MorseLink opens the hotspot settings for you to enable it manually.\n3. If your phone is on Wi-Fi, enabling the hotspot drops the Wi-Fi connection. That is normal.</string>
    <string name="article_slow_title">Transfer is very slow</string>
    <string name="article_slow_body">1. The two transports share one radio: transferring in both directions at once halves each direction — this is expected.\n2. Nearby starts over Bluetooth and escalates to Wi-Fi once the transfer begins; the first seconds are slower.\n3. Keep devices close together and away from heavy interference (microwaves, USB 3 hubs).</string>
    <string name="article_find_title">Files received but I cannot find them</string>
    <string name="article_find_body">Received files land in the Download folder in a MorseLink subfolder (or the folder you chose in Settings), and appear in the History tab. Gallery files are also indexed into the system gallery. If you changed the default location in Settings, look there.</string>

